# SAMPLE FOR A TARGET PROJECT — NOT ACTIVE, NOT THIS REPOSITORY'S CI.
#
# This file is part of the `make` reference Skill's samples (skills/make/samples/ci/).
# It is a template for a project that ADOPTS this Skill's `make verify` (or `make check`)
# command contract — it has nothing to do with, and is never read by, this repository's
# own `.github/workflows/ci.yml`.
#
# It is inert by construction: the `.yml.example` extension is not a GitHub Actions
# workflow filename, and the file does not live under any `.github/workflows/` path,
# so no GitHub Actions runner will ever discover or execute it as-is.
#
# To use it in a target project:
#   1. Copy it to that project's `.github/workflows/verify.yml` (drop `.example`).
#   2. Make sure that project's Makefile actually defines a `verify` target (see this
#      Skill's `references/architecture/command-contracts.md` for the contract shape).
#      If it only defines `check`, change the `run:` line below to `make check`.
#   3. Adjust `runs-on` / triggers / job name to that project's conventions.
#
# What this file is and is not:
#   - It is a pipeline DEFINITION only (what runs). It does NOT configure server-side
#     merge/branch protection (whether a run is REQUIRED to merge) — that is a separate,
#     host-side setting owned by repository/organization admins, described in
#     `references/quality/ci-hooks-and-cache.md`. Enabling this workflow does not, by
#     itself, make it a required status check.
#   - It assumes GNU Make is already present on the runner image (true for GitHub's
#     `ubuntu-latest` image at the time of writing). If the target runner image does not
#     ship `make`, add an explicit install step before the `make verify` step and record
#     the required version, per `references/maintenance/troubleshooting.md`.
#   - It intentionally calls one existing entry point (`make verify`) rather than
#     inlining fmt/lint/test/build commands here, so the hook config, this workflow, and
#     a developer's local invocation all resolve to the same definition (see
#     `references/quality/ci-hooks-and-cache.md`, "One command contract, two callers").
#
# This is a sample, not an endorsement to add CI to any specific project. Adding or
# changing a project's real CI is an `apply`-phase action requiring the project owner's
# explicit approval — see this Skill's SKILL.md and `references/architecture/command-contracts.md`.

name: verify

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:

jobs:
  verify:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Run verify
        run: make verify
